00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
Whether you're tuning hyperparameters or trying out different ideas for learning algorithms

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.840
or just trying out different options for building a machine learning system,

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:13.520
you find that your progress will be much faster if you have a single real number evaluation metric

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:18.240
that lets you quickly tell if the new thing you just tried is working better or worse

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:23.360
than your last idea. So when teams are starting on the machine learning project,

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.640
I often recommend that you set up a single real number evaluation metric for your problem.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:35.760
Let's look at an example. You've heard me say before that applying machine learning is a very empirical

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.680
process. We often have an idea, code it up, run the experiment to see how it did,

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.440
and then use the outcome of the experiment to refine your ideas and then keep going around this

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.480
loop as you keep on improving your algorithm. So let's say for your CAD classifier, you have

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:56.400
previously built some classifier A, and by changing the hyperparameters or the training sets or some

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:04.080
other thing, you've now trained a new classifier B. So one reasonable way to evaluate the performance

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:10.560
of your classifiers is to look at this precision and recall. The exact details of what's precision

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:16.080
and recall don't matter too much for this example, but briefly the definition precision is

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:26.770
of the examples that your classifier recognized as cats, what percentage actually are cats.

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:38.380
So if classifier A has 95% precision, this means that when classifier A says something as a cat,

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:45.500
there's a 95% chance it really is a cat. And recall is of all the images that really are cats,

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:53.340
what percentage were correctly recognized by your classifier? So what percentage of actual cats

00:01:55.940 --> 00:02:08.770
are correctly recognized? So if classifier A has 90% recall, this means that of all of the images

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:14.770
in say your death set that really are cats, classifier A accurately pulled out 90% of them.

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:20.130
So don't worry too much about the definitions of precision and recall, but it turns out that

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:27.570
there's often a trade-off between precision and recall. And you care about both. You want that

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:32.210
when a classifier says something as a cat, there's a high chance it really is a cat. But of all the

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:36.770
images that are cats, you also want it to pull out a large fraction of them as cats.

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:43.250
So it might be reasonable to try to evaluate the classifiers in terms of this precision and this

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:50.850
recall. The problem with using precision recalls the evaluation metric is that if classifier A does

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:58.130
better on recall, which it does here, but classifier B does better on precision, then you're not sure

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:06.290
which classifier is better. And if you're trying out a lot of different ideas, a lot of different

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:12.210
hyperparameters, you want to quickly try out not just two classifiers, but maybe a dozen classifiers

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:20.340
and quickly pick out the quote best ones so you can keep on iterating from there. And with two

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:26.660
evaluation metrics, it's difficult to know how to quickly pick one of the two or quickly pick one

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:33.860
of the ten or one. So what I recommend is rather than using two numbers, precision and recall,

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:40.340
to pick a classifier, you instead define a new evaluation metric that combines precision and recall.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:45.300
In the machine learning literature, the standard way to combine precision and recall is something

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:52.820
called an F1 score, and the details of F1 score aren't too important, but informally you can

00:03:52.820 --> 00:04:02.100
think of this as the average of precision P and recall R. Formally, the F1 score is defined by

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:09.460
dysformulas, 2 over 1 over precision plus 1 over recall. And in mathematics, this is called the

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:17.700
dysfunction, it's called the harmonic mean of precision P and recall R. But less formally,

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:24.020
you can think of this as some way that averages precision and recall. Only instead of taking the

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:28.980
arithmetic mean, you take the harmonic mean, which is defined by dysformulas. And it has

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:34.820
some advantages in terms of trading off precision and recall. But in this example, you can then see

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:42.340
right away that classifier A has a better F1 score, and assuming F1 score is a reasonable way

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:46.900
to combine precision and recall, you can then quickly select classifier A over classifier B.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:51.940
So what I found for a lot of machine learning teams is that having a well-defined dev set,

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:59.700
which is how you're measuring precision and recall, plus a single number evaluation metric,

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:07.860
sometimes I'll call it single real number evaluation metric, allows you to quickly tell

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:14.260
if classifier A or classifier B is better. And therefore, having a dev set plus single number

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:23.920
evaluation metric, this tends to speed up iterating. It speeds up this iterative process

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:29.520
of improving your machine learning algorithm. Let's look at another example. Let's say you're

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:37.920
building a cat app for cat lovers in four major geographies, you know, the US, China, India,

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:43.680
and other, the rest of the world. And let's say that your two classifiers achieve different errors

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:50.960
in these in data from these four different geographies. So algorithm A achieves 3% error

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:58.720
on pictures submitted by US users and so on. So it might be reasonable to keep track of how well

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:04.160
your classifiers do in these different markers or these different geographies. But by tracking four

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.240
numbers, it's very difficult to look at these numbers and quickly decide if algorithm A or

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:14.080
algorithm B is superior. And if you're testing a lot of different classifiers, then it's just

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:18.960
difficult to look at all these numbers and quickly pick one. So what I recommend in this

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:24.560
example is in addition to tracking your performance in the four different geographies

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:30.400
to also compute the average and assuming that average performance is a reasonable single real

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:36.320
number evaluation metric, by computing the average you can quickly tell that it looks like algorithm

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:41.360
C has the lowest average error and we might then go ahead with that one if you have to pick an

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:46.880
algorithm to keep on iterating from. So your workflow in machine learning is often you have

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:51.200
an idea, you implement it and try it out and you want to know whether your idea helped.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:57.200
So what we've seen in this video is that having a single number evaluation metric can really

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:01.200
improve your efficiency or the efficiency of your team in making those decisions.

